00:00:57.240 --> 00:00:59.550
They're offloading data
from their data warehouse

00:00:59.550 --> 00:01:02.220
into Hadoop and other
places, in order

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:04.930
to perform the type of
analysis that they require

00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:06.210
in their business needs.

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:07.530
And this is really because
their data warehouse

00:01:07.530 --> 00:01:09.780
system is no longer fitting
the needs of the business.

00:01:12.355 --> 00:01:16.080
When we look at why many
customers have decided

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:18.075
to move their data
warehouse to BigQuery,

00:01:18.075 --> 00:01:20.040
it's because
BigQuery solves many

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:22.350
of the issues with your
traditional data warehouse

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:23.810
system.

00:01:23.810 --> 00:01:26.630
With BigQuery, you're able
to automate data delivery

00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:28.990
with BigQuery data
transfer service.

00:01:28.990 --> 00:01:33.440
You're able to share queries
among users and people

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:35.090
within your organization.

00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:39.170
You can set the foundations
for AI and real time analytics

00:01:39.170 --> 00:01:41.540
and significantly
reduce the amount

00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:47.090
of effort required to
maintain your data warehouse.

00:01:47.090 --> 00:01:49.510
But one of the major advantages
over the traditional data

00:01:49.510 --> 00:01:53.110
warehouse systems is
BigQuery's architecture.

00:01:53.110 --> 00:01:56.685
Google BigQuery is fully
managed and serverless.

00:01:56.685 --> 00:01:59.140
Storage and compute are
decoupled from each other,

00:01:59.140 --> 00:02:01.810
and they scale
independently and on-demand.

00:02:01.810 --> 00:02:05.350
This offers immense
flexibility and cost

00:02:05.350 --> 00:02:06.920
control for your business.

00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:12.500
So you're not keeping expensive
compute clusters running 24/7.

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:16.120
This is also very different
than the traditional node-based

00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:19.810
cloud data warehouse, or
even on-premise MPP systems.

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:24.420
Some of the secret
sauce of BigQuery

00:02:24.420 --> 00:02:27.400
sits in between the
storage and compute layers

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:29.890
in its in-memory shuffle.

00:02:29.890 --> 00:02:31.980
BigQuery uses remote
memory shuffle

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:36.100
to gain unprecedented query
speed over big data sets.

00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:39.120
This enables you to query
large amounts of data

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:42.060
and get your results very fast.

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:44.520
Each query that you
execute within BigQuery

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:47.230
is split up into a
sequence of stages.

00:02:47.230 --> 00:02:49.740
Each stage is then
read from one worker

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:51.690
to perform some
operation from shuffle

00:02:51.690 --> 00:02:53.970
and then written
back out to shuffle.

00:02:53.970 --> 00:02:57.480
If a worker was to, let's say,
go down because of a hardware

00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:00.010
failure or something like that
during your query processing,

00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:02.010
you wouldn't notice it
because what would happen

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:04.290
is another worker would spin
up and then start reading

00:03:04.290 --> 00:03:07.380
from the previous point that
the previous worker was reading

00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:08.640
from in shuffle.

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:12.000
This offers you great
resilience to downtime.

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:13.920
And it enables you to
continually perform

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:17.010
your analytics
without interruption.

00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:19.950
And then finally when a query
is complete within BigQuery,

00:03:19.950 --> 00:03:23.100
after this reads from shuffle
and writes back to shuffle

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:26.520
and we're sort of done with
the entire query processing,

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:30.330
the results are written
out to persistent storage

00:03:30.330 --> 00:03:31.790
and then returned
back to the user.

00:03:31.790 --> 00:03:34.290
And this persistent
storage enables you to--

00:03:34.290 --> 00:03:36.730
essentially, if you were to
run the same query over again,

00:03:36.730 --> 00:03:39.370
you get the cache of
the previous query.

00:03:39.370 --> 00:03:43.740
So we're able to serve
up consistent results

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:46.790
to the users over time.

00:03:46.790 --> 00:03:50.440
And speaking of storage, one of
the major benefits of BigQuery

00:03:50.440 --> 00:03:52.810
is it's managed storage.

00:03:52.810 --> 00:03:55.660
BigQuery offers durable
and persistent storage

00:03:55.660 --> 00:03:56.950
for your data warehouse.

00:03:56.950 --> 00:04:01.960
And this helps you dramatically
reduce your data operations.

00:04:01.960 --> 00:04:04.862
Gone are the days that your DBAs
are calling you up on the phone

00:04:04.862 --> 00:04:06.820
and saying, hey, we need
you to delete data out

00:04:06.820 --> 00:04:07.737
of the data warehouse.

00:04:07.737 --> 00:04:09.040
We're getting too big.

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:11.440
That was exactly the scenario
in my previous company

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:15.340
where it was a regular basis the
DBA would call us up and tell

00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:16.750
us to start deleting data.

00:04:16.750 --> 00:04:19.250
And we didn't want to delete
data out of our data warehouse,

00:04:19.250 --> 00:04:21.820
but we had resource
constraints and we had to.

00:04:21.820 --> 00:04:24.820
And so with BigQuery,
you're able to ingest

00:04:24.820 --> 00:04:30.890
petabytes of data and basically
throw at it as much as you can.

00:04:30.890 --> 00:04:32.950
And when you throw
data into BigQuery,

00:04:32.950 --> 00:04:36.490
all data is compressed
and encrypted by default.

00:04:36.490 --> 00:04:39.080
This means that
your data is secure,

00:04:39.080 --> 00:04:42.200
once you migrate to BigQuery
and it's in the cloud.

00:04:42.200 --> 00:04:44.500
And not only is it
encrypted, but when

00:04:44.500 --> 00:04:46.180
you save a record
to BigQuery, it's

00:04:46.180 --> 00:04:50.350
not that that record ends up
physically on disk somewhere.

00:04:50.350 --> 00:04:52.870
Those records are split
up into tiny data blocks

00:04:52.870 --> 00:04:56.740
and smeared across thousands of
disks within our data center.

00:04:56.740 --> 00:04:58.600
And each individual
data block is

00:04:58.600 --> 00:05:01.502
encrypted with a different
key encryption key.

00:05:01.502 --> 00:05:03.460
Which means if I went to
the Google data center

00:05:03.460 --> 00:05:05.200
and just pulled
out a disk drive,

00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:08.290
can't read your data because
it's smeared across everything.

00:05:08.290 --> 00:05:12.130
And it also is encrypted
at the data block level.

00:05:12.130 --> 00:05:15.790
And you also don't have to worry
about data replication in DR.

00:05:15.790 --> 00:05:18.160
Because in BigQuery,
when you are running

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:21.550
in a regional configuration,
your data is in multiple zones

00:05:21.550 --> 00:05:22.720
within a region.

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:25.330
When you're running in a
multi-regional configuration,

00:05:25.330 --> 00:05:28.270
your data is spread across
multiple geographically

00:05:28.270 --> 00:05:29.570
distinct regions.

00:05:29.570 --> 00:05:32.080
And so you don't have
to worry about downtime

00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:35.350
from just a region
or zone going down,

00:05:35.350 --> 00:05:37.640
because you will
have that durability

00:05:37.640 --> 00:05:41.622
and consistency across regions.

00:05:41.622 --> 00:05:43.330
Now, the first thing
that you'll probably

00:05:43.330 --> 00:05:46.000
end up doing with
BigQuery, whether it

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:48.700
be in a pre-prod environment
or sandbox environment,

00:05:48.700 --> 00:05:50.710
is loading data.

00:05:50.710 --> 00:05:53.780
With BigQuery batch,
ingest is free.

00:05:53.780 --> 00:05:56.640
It doesn't consume
query capacity.

00:05:56.640 --> 00:05:58.570
And so this is huge,
coming from a lot

00:05:58.570 --> 00:06:00.435
of the traditional
on-premise systems

00:06:00.435 --> 00:06:02.560
where you have to carve
out separate resources just

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:04.590
to deal with batch loading data.

00:06:04.590 --> 00:06:06.730
That doesn't exist
within BigQuery.

00:06:06.730 --> 00:06:09.520
You can load up data
using the communal slots

00:06:09.520 --> 00:06:13.210
available to you, and load
it without having any impact

00:06:13.210 --> 00:06:15.460
on your query capacity.

00:06:15.460 --> 00:06:18.970
When you're loading data,
it has asset semantics.

00:06:18.970 --> 00:06:22.330
This means that I can
replace an entire table that

00:06:22.330 --> 00:06:25.540
may be terabytes in
volume, and the user

00:06:25.540 --> 00:06:29.050
would never know that that
operation is happening.

00:06:29.050 --> 00:06:32.650
Because the operation is
only exposed to the user once

00:06:32.650 --> 00:06:34.090
it's fully complete.

00:06:34.090 --> 00:06:36.730
Or if it fails, it's
never exposed to the user.

00:06:36.730 --> 00:06:38.890
So you don't have
these situations

00:06:38.890 --> 00:06:41.360
where you have to
worry about, oh, I

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:42.620
have users querying the data.

00:06:42.620 --> 00:06:44.260
I can't load at this time.

00:06:44.260 --> 00:06:46.180
And I don't have
situations where

00:06:46.180 --> 00:06:48.580
I have impartial data
loaded, and so people

00:06:48.580 --> 00:06:52.690
are getting bad results when
they're doing their reporting.

00:06:52.690 --> 00:06:54.760
Finally, you can,
like I said earlier,

00:06:54.760 --> 00:06:57.200
load petabytes of data per day.

00:06:57.200 --> 00:06:59.470
There's no real limit
on the amount of data

00:06:59.470 --> 00:07:00.970
that you can load into BigQuery.

00:07:00.970 --> 00:07:04.210
And so a lot of times when
I see customers move over

00:07:04.210 --> 00:07:06.790
to BigQuery, they may be
moving over with, let's say,

00:07:06.790 --> 00:07:08.050
100 terabytes.

00:07:08.050 --> 00:07:11.440
But then they end up
using substantially more

00:07:11.440 --> 00:07:13.840
than that on BigQuery
because of pent up demand

00:07:13.840 --> 00:07:15.130
within their organization.

00:07:15.130 --> 00:07:19.840
And they're able to just scale
as necessary to deal with that.

00:07:19.840 --> 00:07:22.700
And then finally, there's a
streaming API for real time.

00:07:22.700 --> 00:07:26.590
So if I needed to ingest real
time records or near real time,

00:07:26.590 --> 00:07:29.620
I can stream thousands
to hundreds of thousands

00:07:29.620 --> 00:07:32.650
of records into BigQuery,
without any impact

00:07:32.650 --> 00:07:35.800
to my query performance.

00:07:35.800 --> 00:07:38.470
Which actually brings us
to our first best practice.

00:07:38.470 --> 00:07:41.070
So BigQuery supports
a wide variety

00:07:41.070 --> 00:07:43.470
of file formats
for data ingestion.

00:07:43.470 --> 00:07:46.650
Some are going to be
naturally faster than others.

00:07:46.650 --> 00:07:49.530
When optimizing for
load speed, prefer

00:07:49.530 --> 00:07:51.780
using the Avro file format.

00:07:51.780 --> 00:07:54.330
Avro is a binary,
row-based format,

00:07:54.330 --> 00:07:58.410
which enables us to split it
and then read it in parallel

00:07:58.410 --> 00:08:00.220
with multiple workers.

00:08:00.220 --> 00:08:02.320
In fact, in a recent
test with a customer,

00:08:02.320 --> 00:08:04.410
we found that Avro with
deflate compression

00:08:04.410 --> 00:08:08.940
to be an order of magnitude
faster than the other methods

00:08:08.940 --> 00:08:11.380
of loading data into BigQuery.

00:08:11.380 --> 00:08:14.490
Now, Parquet and ORC are
also binary-based formats.

00:08:14.490 --> 00:08:17.580
And they're relatively
fast for loading data.

00:08:17.580 --> 00:08:20.130
When I'm talking to
customers, they often

00:08:20.130 --> 00:08:22.838
think that since Parquet and
ORC are their columnar formats,

00:08:22.838 --> 00:08:24.630
and I'm loading into
managed storage, which

00:08:24.630 --> 00:08:28.313
is also columnar, that should
be the fastest way to load in.

00:08:28.313 --> 00:08:29.730
But when we're
loading data, we're

00:08:29.730 --> 00:08:32.130
actually taking the
entirety of the record.

00:08:32.130 --> 00:08:34.740
So we're not taking advantage
of any of the columnar format.

00:08:34.740 --> 00:08:36.450
And there's a lot
of random reads.

00:08:36.450 --> 00:08:39.970
So loading data via
Parquet and ORC is fast,

00:08:39.970 --> 00:08:43.679
but not going to be as fast
as loading data from Avro.

00:08:43.679 --> 00:08:45.190
And then finally
here at the bottom,

00:08:45.190 --> 00:08:49.020
we have CSV and JSON compressed.

00:08:49.020 --> 00:08:51.210
Loading data from
compressed files,

00:08:51.210 --> 00:08:53.610
specifically CSV
and JSON, is going

00:08:53.610 --> 00:08:56.940
to be slower than loading
data in any other format.

00:08:56.940 --> 00:09:01.110
And the reason being is because,
since the compression of Gzip

00:09:01.110 --> 00:09:04.080
is not splittable, we
have to take that file,

00:09:04.080 --> 00:09:06.390
load it onto a slot
within BigQuery,

00:09:06.390 --> 00:09:09.690
and then do the decompression,
and then finally parallelize

00:09:09.690 --> 00:09:11.500
the load afterwards.

00:09:11.500 --> 00:09:13.990
So there's going to
be more overhead,

00:09:13.990 --> 00:09:17.145
and it'll be slower than loading
data from these other formats.

00:09:20.610 --> 00:09:23.010
And after I loaded
data into BigQuery,

00:09:23.010 --> 00:09:24.690
I'm probably thinking
about, how am

00:09:24.690 --> 00:09:28.200
I going to do transformations
within my system?

00:09:28.200 --> 00:09:30.990
Should I be doing ELT or ETL?

00:09:30.990 --> 00:09:33.210
What is the best practice there?

00:09:33.210 --> 00:09:36.840
So in general, you want
to prefer ELT over ETL

00:09:36.840 --> 00:09:38.160
where possible.

00:09:38.160 --> 00:09:40.740
BigQuery is very
scalable and can

00:09:40.740 --> 00:09:44.650
handle large transformations
on a ton of data.

00:09:44.650 --> 00:09:46.500
So generally, when
customers think

00:09:46.500 --> 00:09:49.500
about doing ELT versus
ETL, ELT is where they're

00:09:49.500 --> 00:09:50.802
going to start within BigQuery.

00:09:50.802 --> 00:09:53.010
It's also quite a bit simpler,
because you could just

00:09:53.010 --> 00:09:55.230
write some SQL queries,
transform some data,

00:09:55.230 --> 00:09:57.120
and then move data
around between tables,

00:09:57.120 --> 00:10:01.950
and not have to worry about
managing a separate ETL

00:10:01.950 --> 00:10:04.480
application.

00:10:04.480 --> 00:10:07.000
And then as you're loading
data in, what you may find

00:10:07.000 --> 00:10:10.660
is that, well, I have
a CSV file maybe.

00:10:10.660 --> 00:10:13.640
And the date isn't really
in a standard format.

00:10:13.640 --> 00:10:16.990
How do I get this into the
data type within BigQuery?

00:10:16.990 --> 00:10:19.510
Well, you can have separate
scripts or ETL processes

00:10:19.510 --> 00:10:22.000
which process that and
pre-process it before loading

00:10:22.000 --> 00:10:23.530
it into BigQuery.

00:10:23.530 --> 00:10:27.520
But you can also leverage
federated queries onto GCS

00:10:27.520 --> 00:10:30.405
to transform that data and
load it in a single step.

00:10:30.405 --> 00:10:31.780
Now, the only
downside of this is

00:10:31.780 --> 00:10:33.838
this will consume
your query capacity.

00:10:33.838 --> 00:10:35.380
But it enables you
to keep everything

00:10:35.380 --> 00:10:38.500
in SQL, if you just have a bunch
of one off use cases for this.

00:10:41.070 --> 00:10:43.890
Once you've started
loading data into BigQuery,

00:10:43.890 --> 00:10:45.780
in general, within
your warehouse,

00:10:45.780 --> 00:10:47.820
you're going to
want to leverage raw

00:10:47.820 --> 00:10:52.500
and staging tables before
publishing to reporting tables.

00:10:52.500 --> 00:10:55.140
The raw table essentially
contains the full daily

00:10:55.140 --> 00:11:00.000
extract, or a full load of
the data that they're loading.

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:02.250
The staging table
then is basically

00:11:02.250 --> 00:11:04.500
your change data capture table.

00:11:04.500 --> 00:11:06.990
So you can utilize
queries or DML

00:11:06.990 --> 00:11:09.870
to merge that data
into your staging table

00:11:09.870 --> 00:11:13.830
and have a full history of all
the data that's being inserted.

00:11:13.830 --> 00:11:15.680
And then finally,
your reporting tables

00:11:15.680 --> 00:11:18.180
are going to be the ones that
you publish out to your users.

00:11:18.180 --> 00:11:19.972
And your users are
going to execute queries

00:11:19.972 --> 00:11:20.850
against these tables.

00:11:20.850 --> 00:11:23.280
And they may be dimensionalized
in a star schema,

00:11:23.280 --> 00:11:27.870
or they may be denormalized,
depending on the use case.

00:11:27.870 --> 00:11:30.570
And that method of
going from staging

00:11:30.570 --> 00:11:32.877
to reporting using
MERGE statements,

00:11:32.877 --> 00:11:34.710
that's where you can
perform your SCE Type 2

00:11:34.710 --> 00:11:36.070
transformations.

00:11:36.070 --> 00:11:38.940
So if I have the
history of records

00:11:38.940 --> 00:11:40.710
all within my
staging table, I can

00:11:40.710 --> 00:11:43.950
easily within my MERGE
statement mark a record

00:11:43.950 --> 00:11:52.448
as expired, and then set the
new date for the current record.

00:11:52.448 --> 00:11:53.990
And then finally,
when you're getting

00:11:53.990 --> 00:11:58.910
into streaming loads or
really complex batch loads,

00:11:58.910 --> 00:12:02.420
you can leverage data
flow or data fusion

00:12:02.420 --> 00:12:04.200
to speed up those pipelines.

00:12:04.200 --> 00:12:06.290
So oftentimes when I
work with customers,

00:12:06.290 --> 00:12:08.510
they're starting
out with the ELT

00:12:08.510 --> 00:12:10.430
as their primary
mechanism of loading

00:12:10.430 --> 00:12:12.530
and transforming
data into BigQuery.

00:12:12.530 --> 00:12:15.260
But then they may have
some really gnarly batch

00:12:15.260 --> 00:12:18.848
process that doesn't really
fit into SQL cleanly.

00:12:18.848 --> 00:12:21.140
And this is where they'll
sort of scale themselves out.

00:12:21.140 --> 00:12:23.900
They'll start utilizing
data flow or data fusion

00:12:23.900 --> 00:12:26.870
to speed up those loads
and transformations

00:12:26.870 --> 00:12:30.330
and do more complex
activities on that data.

00:12:30.330 --> 00:12:32.380
And if you're getting
started with streaming,

00:12:32.380 --> 00:12:34.700
I recommend using the
Google-provided dataflow

00:12:34.700 --> 00:12:35.690
templates.

00:12:35.690 --> 00:12:37.640
So Google provides
dataflow templates

00:12:37.640 --> 00:12:40.880
for loading data from
multiple different places

00:12:40.880 --> 00:12:42.680
and moving data around.

00:12:42.680 --> 00:12:44.270
You can find those
dataflow templates

00:12:44.270 --> 00:12:46.100
within the Dataflow UI.

00:12:46.100 --> 00:12:49.650
So within the Create Job
from Template button,

00:12:49.650 --> 00:12:51.590
you'll find all these
dataflow templates.

00:12:51.590 --> 00:12:55.070
And if you find that it
mostly fits your use case,

00:12:55.070 --> 00:12:57.530
but I want to make one
slight modification,

00:12:57.530 --> 00:12:59.450
all those templates
are also open sourced.

00:12:59.450 --> 00:13:01.430
So you can go to the
Open Source repo.

00:13:01.430 --> 00:13:03.750
You can modify the
code to fit your needs,

00:13:03.750 --> 00:13:07.190
whether it be adding some
windowing in, or just using it

00:13:07.190 --> 00:13:09.150
as a baseline for
your own pipelines,

00:13:09.150 --> 00:13:12.727
and then quickly accelerate
your development.

00:13:15.350 --> 00:13:17.740
And this is sort of like
an end-to-end example

00:13:17.740 --> 00:13:21.310
of change data capture
coming into BigQuery.

00:13:21.310 --> 00:13:25.720
So with some customer
submits, they may have Oracle.

00:13:25.720 --> 00:13:29.560
And we'll be capturing the
changes in Golden Gate.

00:13:29.560 --> 00:13:32.350
We'll publish those records
to either Pub/Sub or Kafka,

00:13:32.350 --> 00:13:34.630
to ingest those
records in real time.

00:13:34.630 --> 00:13:37.210
For doing batch loads, we may
just load that data into GCS

00:13:37.210 --> 00:13:40.750
as our landing zone, before
loading it into BigQuery.

00:13:40.750 --> 00:13:44.980
And then we'll utilize dataflow
or BigQuery batch loads to get

00:13:44.980 --> 00:13:47.093
that data into our raw tables.

00:13:47.093 --> 00:13:48.760
Then once the data
is in our raw tables,

00:13:48.760 --> 00:13:51.940
we can utilize Cloud
Composer to schedule queries

00:13:51.940 --> 00:13:55.780
within our BigQuery system
to move that data from raw

00:13:55.780 --> 00:13:58.870
to our change log, then
dimensionalizing for our star

00:13:58.870 --> 00:14:00.630
schema.

00:14:00.630 --> 00:14:02.320
And this is a very
standard process

00:14:02.320 --> 00:14:04.960
that I see a lot of
customers utilizing.

00:14:07.650 --> 00:14:10.037
Now, once we have a
strategy for loading data,

00:14:10.037 --> 00:14:11.620
it's time for us to
think about, well,

00:14:11.620 --> 00:14:13.155
how do we want to
design our schema?

00:14:16.550 --> 00:14:18.980
In general, I find
that customers will

00:14:18.980 --> 00:14:21.915
overthink this step upfront.

00:14:21.915 --> 00:14:24.290
They'll think about like,
well, let's redesign everything

00:14:24.290 --> 00:14:24.880
for BigQuery.

00:14:24.880 --> 00:14:26.040
It's a new system.

00:14:26.040 --> 00:14:28.185
Maybe I need to
denormalize everything.

00:14:28.185 --> 00:14:29.560
But in general,
the best practice

00:14:29.560 --> 00:14:32.390
is actually to migrate your
star schema and snowflake schema

00:14:32.390 --> 00:14:35.820
as is, and then
optimize as necessary.

00:14:35.820 --> 00:14:39.440
This enables you to
find what may not run

00:14:39.440 --> 00:14:41.810
as is quickly within BigQuery.

00:14:41.810 --> 00:14:46.520
And then optimize it after
identifying those gaps.

00:14:46.520 --> 00:14:49.260
Denormalization is
not a requirement,

00:14:49.260 --> 00:14:52.370
but it can speed up
slow analytical queries

00:14:52.370 --> 00:14:55.400
by reducing the amount of
data that we have to shuffle.

00:14:55.400 --> 00:14:56.960
So to give you an
example, there is

00:14:56.960 --> 00:14:58.627
one customer that I
was working with who

00:14:58.627 --> 00:15:00.830
had 77 joins in a query.

00:15:00.830 --> 00:15:03.580
I was impressed that they
were actually executing

00:15:03.580 --> 00:15:06.400
a query with 77 joins.

00:15:06.400 --> 00:15:09.590
And what we found is that,
well, you know, a lot of this

00:15:09.590 --> 00:15:12.260
did not need to be
a join statement.

00:15:12.260 --> 00:15:15.270
A lot of this was
like a country field,

00:15:15.270 --> 00:15:16.818
or it was a date dimension.

00:15:16.818 --> 00:15:18.860
It was stuff that could
be naturally denormalized

00:15:18.860 --> 00:15:20.220
into the output table.

00:15:20.220 --> 00:15:22.940
And so we were able to reduce
the 77 joins that they were

00:15:22.940 --> 00:15:25.580
doing down to four
joins, which made

00:15:25.580 --> 00:15:29.430
it a lot more scalable as
their data volume grew.

00:15:29.430 --> 00:15:31.440
And speaking about
denormalizing,

00:15:31.440 --> 00:15:36.390
BigQuery has capabilities for
nested and repeated fields.

00:15:36.390 --> 00:15:39.270
And this enables you to store
semi-structured data very

00:15:39.270 --> 00:15:43.170
naturally within BigQuery,
and then query over it.

00:15:43.170 --> 00:15:46.890
Now oftentimes, this is
a very different concept

00:15:46.890 --> 00:15:49.873
than people used to
row-based architectures

00:15:49.873 --> 00:15:52.290
where they're not necessarily
storing semi-structured data

00:15:52.290 --> 00:15:53.610
within their data warehouse.

00:15:53.610 --> 00:15:56.850
So for leveraging nested
and repeated fields,

00:15:56.850 --> 00:15:58.820
I generally have
three guidelines.

00:15:58.820 --> 00:16:02.460
One, leverage them when you have
tightly coupled or immutable

00:16:02.460 --> 00:16:03.720
relationships.

00:16:03.720 --> 00:16:07.485
If I have an order and I have
an order line items table,

00:16:07.485 --> 00:16:09.750
the line items will
never really be

00:16:09.750 --> 00:16:11.330
used without the order table.

00:16:11.330 --> 00:16:14.610
And these are naturally
coupled, oftentimes immutable.

00:16:14.610 --> 00:16:18.520
And I'll denormalize
that into a single table.

00:16:18.520 --> 00:16:21.210
So as opposed to having the
order in the order line items

00:16:21.210 --> 00:16:23.670
table, I can have
an orders table

00:16:23.670 --> 00:16:26.660
that has a list of line items.

00:16:26.660 --> 00:16:32.240
Similar ways to do that
is sessions with events.

00:16:32.240 --> 00:16:34.460
So an event within
a session I can

00:16:34.460 --> 00:16:37.370
have, as opposed to having a
separate table for sessions

00:16:37.370 --> 00:16:38.210
and events.

00:16:38.210 --> 00:16:41.420
I could have a list of
events within a session

00:16:41.420 --> 00:16:44.850
record in a sessions table.

00:16:44.850 --> 00:16:47.220
I'll also think about
using this for infrequently

00:16:47.220 --> 00:16:51.270
changing data-- so country,
region, date dimension.

00:16:51.270 --> 00:16:53.460
Hopefully, these are
un-frequently changing.

00:16:53.460 --> 00:16:56.220
And so this is stuff that
you can actually just

00:16:56.220 --> 00:16:59.520
merge into your
reporting table and have

00:16:59.520 --> 00:17:02.130
it be slightly more
denormalized and easier

00:17:02.130 --> 00:17:04.780
to query for the users.

00:17:04.780 --> 00:17:06.690
And then finally,
simplifying queries.

00:17:06.690 --> 00:17:10.560
A lot of times, queries for
users will be super complex.

00:17:10.560 --> 00:17:13.079
And just by nesting
in repeated fields,

00:17:13.079 --> 00:17:17.339
you can sufficiently
decrease the complexity

00:17:17.339 --> 00:17:18.720
of those tables and the usage.

00:17:21.684 --> 00:17:25.640
Which brings us to our next
best practice-- partitioning.

00:17:25.640 --> 00:17:27.950
Partitions in
BigQuery physically

00:17:27.950 --> 00:17:32.030
split your data on disk,
based on ingestion time,

00:17:32.030 --> 00:17:35.180
or based on a column
within your data.

00:17:35.180 --> 00:17:38.600
As seen within the diagram,
each column of data

00:17:38.600 --> 00:17:41.660
is split according
to the partition key.

00:17:41.660 --> 00:17:43.790
And then queries over
that partition key,

00:17:43.790 --> 00:17:46.430
as opposed to scanning the
entirety of that column

00:17:46.430 --> 00:17:48.410
and all data within
that column, we're

00:17:48.410 --> 00:17:51.170
able to do partition
elimination and only scan

00:17:51.170 --> 00:17:55.230
the data that's necessary to
answer that query question.

00:17:55.230 --> 00:17:58.750
And this provides huge
cost benefits, but also

00:17:58.750 --> 00:18:00.820
huge performance
benefits, especially

00:18:00.820 --> 00:18:03.810
on large fact tables.

00:18:03.810 --> 00:18:05.730
So as a best
practice, whenever you

00:18:05.730 --> 00:18:07.920
have a fact table
or temporal table,

00:18:07.920 --> 00:18:12.573
utilize a partition column
on your date dimension.

00:18:12.573 --> 00:18:16.970
And as your users are using
these tables, oftentimes,

00:18:16.970 --> 00:18:19.390
what I've found is
users will forget

00:18:19.390 --> 00:18:21.040
to supply a partition key.

00:18:21.040 --> 00:18:25.390
So they won't get the benefits
of the performance of partition

00:18:25.390 --> 00:18:26.740
elimination.

00:18:26.740 --> 00:18:30.190
BigQuery has an option in which
you can require a partition

00:18:30.190 --> 00:18:33.760
filter on the table,
which basically means

00:18:33.760 --> 00:18:36.250
that if I'm a user,
and I execute a query,

00:18:36.250 --> 00:18:38.820
and I don't specify
an as-of-date filter,

00:18:38.820 --> 00:18:40.810
and if I am partitioned
by as-of-date,

00:18:40.810 --> 00:18:44.240
then my query will return back
an error and basically say,

00:18:44.240 --> 00:18:47.890
I didn't specify a
required partition filter.

00:18:47.890 --> 00:18:51.400
And this enables you
to avoid inadvertently

00:18:51.400 --> 00:18:55.480
scanning large amounts of data,
to essentially keep your costs

00:18:55.480 --> 00:18:59.498
down and avoid errant queries by
users who may not know better,

00:18:59.498 --> 00:19:01.540
until they're actually
presented with that error.

00:19:04.880 --> 00:19:08.630
Clustering-- clustering allows
you to physically order data

00:19:08.630 --> 00:19:10.430
within a partition.

00:19:10.430 --> 00:19:13.890
So you can do clustering
by one or multiple keys.

00:19:13.890 --> 00:19:17.690
So if I had a
partition of 2018603,

00:19:17.690 --> 00:19:19.610
I can physically order
the data for a user

00:19:19.610 --> 00:19:22.190
ID in that partition.

00:19:22.190 --> 00:19:24.070
And that way when the
query is executing,

00:19:24.070 --> 00:19:28.900
we're only scanning a very
small subset of that partition.

00:19:28.900 --> 00:19:31.050
And this can provide
massive performance benefits

00:19:31.050 --> 00:19:32.910
when used properly.

00:19:32.910 --> 00:19:34.410
You're going to
want to prefer using

00:19:34.410 --> 00:19:37.170
clustering on common
filtered columns,

00:19:37.170 --> 00:19:38.620
especially within
your fact table.

00:19:38.620 --> 00:19:42.000
So if you had, let's say, a
sales fact table or transaction

00:19:42.000 --> 00:19:43.920
fact table, you may
want to partition it

00:19:43.920 --> 00:19:45.510
based on transaction date.

00:19:45.510 --> 00:19:48.690
But you may want to
cluster it on, let's say,

00:19:48.690 --> 00:19:51.410
region, or store,
or customer ID.

00:19:51.410 --> 00:19:54.630
And this will enable you
to have better performing

00:19:54.630 --> 00:19:56.640
queries over that table.

00:19:56.640 --> 00:20:00.150
Now, you can only have one
clustering key per table.

00:20:00.150 --> 00:20:04.290
So it's basically most customers
will look at the queries

00:20:04.290 --> 00:20:06.450
that they are running
and then determine

00:20:06.450 --> 00:20:08.157
what is the best
clustering key that

00:20:08.157 --> 00:20:10.740
will satisfy most queries that
are running against this table.

00:20:13.430 --> 00:20:15.470
When you're filtering
on clustering columns,

00:20:15.470 --> 00:20:17.940
you want to filter in the
order that they're specified.

00:20:17.940 --> 00:20:22.490
So when you specify, let's
say, on that transaction table

00:20:22.490 --> 00:20:25.140
that I have partitioned
by transaction date

00:20:25.140 --> 00:20:27.020
and that I'm
clustering by, let's

00:20:27.020 --> 00:20:29.360
say, region and then
store, if I was just

00:20:29.360 --> 00:20:31.760
to provide a store
filter to my query,

00:20:31.760 --> 00:20:34.050
then I'm not taking
advantage of clustering.

00:20:34.050 --> 00:20:37.280
So you want to order your
clustering keys properly

00:20:37.280 --> 00:20:40.070
so that it can essentially
limit the amount of data

00:20:40.070 --> 00:20:42.520
scans per query.

00:20:42.520 --> 00:20:46.830
And then finally, most
people will ask me,

00:20:46.830 --> 00:20:51.440
how do I do clustering
on a table that

00:20:51.440 --> 00:20:52.605
has no date dimension?

00:20:52.605 --> 00:20:54.230
So let's say I have
a dimensional table

00:20:54.230 --> 00:20:58.250
and its product or its customer.

00:20:58.250 --> 00:21:01.310
And I just want to cluster
on that that customer ID

00:21:01.310 --> 00:21:03.050
or product ID.

00:21:03.050 --> 00:21:04.970
Well, you can utilize
natural partitioning

00:21:04.970 --> 00:21:08.943
on that table and cluster
based on that column.

00:21:08.943 --> 00:21:10.610
Even though the
partition means nothing,

00:21:10.610 --> 00:21:13.110
it'll still provide
performance benefits.

00:21:13.110 --> 00:21:16.430
You can also utilize a fake
date column within your table,

00:21:16.430 --> 00:21:19.640
and then cluster on
customer ID or product ID.

00:21:19.640 --> 00:21:21.890
And that'll provide
even better performance

00:21:21.890 --> 00:21:24.230
because everything is going
to end up on one partition.

00:21:24.230 --> 00:21:27.240
And then all the data is going
to be ordered within there.

00:21:27.240 --> 00:21:31.520
And then finally, this is
sort of a workaround today.

00:21:31.520 --> 00:21:35.240
If you had a use case where
you have a dimensional table

00:21:35.240 --> 00:21:38.000
and you have maybe an
integer key, in alpha,

00:21:38.000 --> 00:21:41.497
BigQuery has support for
integer range partitioning.

00:21:41.497 --> 00:21:43.580
And so if you're interested
in that functionality,

00:21:43.580 --> 00:21:45.997
you can sign up for that alpha
and begin testing that out.

00:21:49.150 --> 00:21:51.190
When customers are
coming from systems

00:21:51.190 --> 00:21:55.870
which have sequence generators,
there are oftentimes

00:21:55.870 --> 00:22:01.170
questions of, how do I handle
surrogate keys in BigQuery?

00:22:01.170 --> 00:22:04.390
BigQuery doesn't have
any sequence generator.

00:22:04.390 --> 00:22:07.030
And their first attempt
at this will often

00:22:07.030 --> 00:22:08.740
be, well, I'll use row number.

00:22:08.740 --> 00:22:10.870
I'm doing ELT, I'm
going to use row number.

00:22:10.870 --> 00:22:14.550
And that's going to give me
my sequence over my table.

00:22:14.550 --> 00:22:16.780
But that's not a
scalable solution

00:22:16.780 --> 00:22:18.910
because, when you're using
row number in that way,

00:22:18.910 --> 00:22:20.577
everything within the
query is ending up

00:22:20.577 --> 00:22:21.730
on a single partition.

00:22:21.730 --> 00:22:24.690
And then you're doing
basically a sort of it

00:22:24.690 --> 00:22:27.270
could be millions or
billions of records.

00:22:27.270 --> 00:22:29.020
A more scalable
way to do this is

00:22:29.020 --> 00:22:34.600
to prefer UUIDs in
place of surrogate keys.

00:22:34.600 --> 00:22:36.520
And then, if you have
the use case where

00:22:36.520 --> 00:22:39.490
you need a deterministic
surrogate key,

00:22:39.490 --> 00:22:43.040
you can use a hashing
function, such as SHA-256,

00:22:43.040 --> 00:22:48.067
which enables you to hash over
one or multiple business keys

00:22:48.067 --> 00:22:49.900
and so that you can
have a deterministic key

00:22:49.900 --> 00:22:53.370
be generated every time.

00:22:53.370 --> 00:22:55.860
Now, once I've loaded
data into BigQuery,

00:22:55.860 --> 00:22:58.560
I've designed a schema,
I may be worried

00:22:58.560 --> 00:23:01.290
about how to manage
workloads once users

00:23:01.290 --> 00:23:04.597
start using the system.

00:23:04.597 --> 00:23:06.430
But before we talk about
workload management

00:23:06.430 --> 00:23:09.040
within BigQuery, it's
important to understand

00:23:09.040 --> 00:23:12.070
how BigQuery executes queries.

00:23:12.070 --> 00:23:14.890
BigQuery has a fair scheduler.

00:23:14.890 --> 00:23:18.100
What this means is that if I was
to execute a query in BigQuery,

00:23:18.100 --> 00:23:20.590
just a single query,
it has full access

00:23:20.590 --> 00:23:22.760
to the resources
available to it.

00:23:22.760 --> 00:23:26.620
So in this case, it's a
project that has 2,000 slots.

00:23:26.620 --> 00:23:29.860
So that query has full
access to those 2,000 slots.

00:23:29.860 --> 00:23:32.140
Now, it may not use
those 2,000 slots

00:23:32.140 --> 00:23:35.320
because the number of slots
that each stage requires

00:23:35.320 --> 00:23:37.810
is based on the number of
parallel inputs to that stage,

00:23:37.810 --> 00:23:40.870
but it still has access
to the full 2,000 slots.

00:23:40.870 --> 00:23:44.230
Now, when I execute two
queries within BigQuery,

00:23:44.230 --> 00:23:46.430
we subdivide those resources.

00:23:46.430 --> 00:23:50.080
So as opposed to that one query
having access to 2,000 slots,

00:23:50.080 --> 00:23:51.820
now we have two queries
that have access

00:23:51.820 --> 00:23:53.860
to 1,000 slots each.

00:23:53.860 --> 00:23:56.620
And then so on and so forth,
as you execute more queries,

00:23:56.620 --> 00:24:00.010
the resources continually
get subdivided.

00:24:00.010 --> 00:24:03.050
Now, there's a common
question there is, well,

00:24:03.050 --> 00:24:06.100
how do I prioritize
between queries?

00:24:06.100 --> 00:24:07.780
Maybe there's some
queries that I

00:24:07.780 --> 00:24:10.090
care a lot more
about that I don't

00:24:10.090 --> 00:24:12.760
want to have necessarily
equal resources available

00:24:12.760 --> 00:24:16.385
to as much as my other queries.

00:24:16.385 --> 00:24:18.010
So this is where you
can take advantage

00:24:18.010 --> 00:24:20.830
of BigQuery reservations.

00:24:20.830 --> 00:24:23.680
BigQuery reservations allow
you to create reservations

00:24:23.680 --> 00:24:27.280
of slots, assign projects
to those reservations,

00:24:27.280 --> 00:24:29.830
so you can allocate
more or less resources

00:24:29.830 --> 00:24:31.940
to certain types of queries.

00:24:31.940 --> 00:24:34.360
So in this case, I have
a dashboarding project,

00:24:34.360 --> 00:24:37.330
I have a data science project,
and I have an ETL project.

00:24:37.330 --> 00:24:39.220
I may want to
allocate more slots

00:24:39.220 --> 00:24:41.860
to my dashboarding project,
because those dashboards are

00:24:41.860 --> 00:24:44.200
being utilized by
my C-level execs.

00:24:44.200 --> 00:24:47.490
And I want to make sure that the
response time is always quick.

00:24:47.490 --> 00:24:50.660
And I don't really care about my
ETL project or my data science

00:24:50.660 --> 00:24:51.160
project.

00:24:51.160 --> 00:24:54.020
They can sort of get
results as needed.

00:24:54.020 --> 00:24:55.930
And this allows me
to sort of prioritize

00:24:55.930 --> 00:24:58.540
between the different
workloads that I'm

00:24:58.540 --> 00:25:00.350
executing within BigQuery.

00:25:00.350 --> 00:25:03.490
And an important thing
here is that by carving out

00:25:03.490 --> 00:25:06.400
resources in this way, you're
not carving out data silos.

00:25:06.400 --> 00:25:09.850
Because idle slots
are seamlessly

00:25:09.850 --> 00:25:11.590
distributed between
the different nodes

00:25:11.590 --> 00:25:12.940
within your reservation.

00:25:12.940 --> 00:25:15.730
So let's say that my
dashboarding project is not

00:25:15.730 --> 00:25:17.500
using 1,000 slots.

00:25:17.500 --> 00:25:19.690
That means that any other
project within my system

00:25:19.690 --> 00:25:21.280
can take advantage of it.

00:25:21.280 --> 00:25:23.080
These aren't physical
resources that you're

00:25:23.080 --> 00:25:26.600
sort of segmenting
off and putting away.

00:25:26.600 --> 00:25:30.130
And if you have low
utilization, then you're

00:25:30.130 --> 00:25:32.800
just paying for compute
that you're not using.

00:25:32.800 --> 00:25:35.530
You're able to efficiently
distribute these slots

00:25:35.530 --> 00:25:37.570
across all these
reservations, but also

00:25:37.570 --> 00:25:40.870
have that priority so that if a
dashboarding query did come in,

00:25:40.870 --> 00:25:43.630
it sort of preempts
the other projects

00:25:43.630 --> 00:25:47.660
and is able to take advantage
of those full 1,000 slots.

00:25:47.660 --> 00:25:49.660
And this is also where
the separation of storage

00:25:49.660 --> 00:25:51.760
and compute comes in handy.

00:25:51.760 --> 00:25:54.432
Because we're segmenting
off compute resources,

00:25:54.432 --> 00:25:56.140
but we don't have to
think about storage.

00:25:56.140 --> 00:25:58.630
Storage still exists
somewhere in some project.

00:25:58.630 --> 00:26:01.930
And any project and any user can
access that storage, provided

00:26:01.930 --> 00:26:04.960
that their permission to that
storage via our standard ACLs

00:26:04.960 --> 00:26:06.058
on data sets.

00:26:08.677 --> 00:26:10.510
So this brings us to
another best practices,

00:26:10.510 --> 00:26:13.370
to segment your
users based on roles.

00:26:13.370 --> 00:26:16.960
So as opposed to having
everybody in one project

00:26:16.960 --> 00:26:20.020
accessing BigQuery, the same
projects which you're storing

00:26:20.020 --> 00:26:23.650
all your data, segment out your
users into different projects

00:26:23.650 --> 00:26:26.060
based on their role
within your organization.

00:26:26.060 --> 00:26:30.610
So I may have a separate project
for my data science team,

00:26:30.610 --> 00:26:34.360
or a department's data science
team, and a separate project

00:26:34.360 --> 00:26:36.110
for my data analysts.

00:26:36.110 --> 00:26:39.580
And then if I needed to start
doing that prioritization

00:26:39.580 --> 00:26:41.110
among the different
groups, I can

00:26:41.110 --> 00:26:42.850
do that very naturally
just by assigning

00:26:42.850 --> 00:26:45.183
their projects to different
nodes within the reservation

00:26:45.183 --> 00:26:45.730
hierarchy.

00:26:48.610 --> 00:26:52.030
So once I have
workloads executing,

00:26:52.030 --> 00:26:56.030
I probably want to know
what's going on in my system.

00:26:56.030 --> 00:27:00.440
BigQuery logs all admin
activity and data access

00:27:00.440 --> 00:27:02.060
into Stackdriver Logging.

00:27:02.060 --> 00:27:05.000
So you're able to access
all the logs of what

00:27:05.000 --> 00:27:10.900
is going on within your system
and do analysis over them.

00:27:10.900 --> 00:27:12.970
But in most cases,
customers will

00:27:12.970 --> 00:27:15.460
want to do analysis
over that in BigQuery.

00:27:15.460 --> 00:27:17.710
And so as a best
practice, we actually

00:27:17.710 --> 00:27:20.830
say, export your data access
logs back into BigQuery.

00:27:20.830 --> 00:27:22.780
That way, you can query
over those data access

00:27:22.780 --> 00:27:24.940
logs, and you can
have those data access

00:27:24.940 --> 00:27:26.590
logs in a single place.

00:27:26.590 --> 00:27:29.410
So you could have thousands
of projects using BigQuery.

00:27:29.410 --> 00:27:32.050
But if you use an
aggregated export of those

00:27:32.050 --> 00:27:34.660
logs to a single
BigQuery project,

00:27:34.660 --> 00:27:37.330
then all that data is going
to exist in a single place.

00:27:37.330 --> 00:27:40.060
All that audit data for
your entire organization

00:27:40.060 --> 00:27:42.430
in a single data set
and easily queryable

00:27:42.430 --> 00:27:46.240
by people who are
interested in knowing

00:27:46.240 --> 00:27:49.360
what's exactly going on.

00:27:49.360 --> 00:27:54.510
And then finally, as a best
practice, is go the extra mile

00:27:54.510 --> 00:27:57.960
and visualize your data
audit logs in Data Studio.

00:27:57.960 --> 00:28:02.520
By visualizing your audit logs,
you can analyze spending trends

00:28:02.520 --> 00:28:06.210
and query volume over time and
break down costs by project,

00:28:06.210 --> 00:28:10.830
by user, and be proactive about
tracking expensive queries.

00:28:10.830 --> 00:28:14.910
So I actually have
a short demo here.

00:28:19.770 --> 00:28:20.450
There we are.

00:28:20.450 --> 00:28:23.350
So this is an audit
dashboard that I

00:28:23.350 --> 00:28:28.180
built on top of the audit
logs within my project, right?

00:28:28.180 --> 00:28:31.060
So using the audit
logs, I'm able to get

00:28:31.060 --> 00:28:33.430
at slot utilization over time.

00:28:33.430 --> 00:28:39.070
I'm able to estimate the cost of
the queries that I'm executing.

00:28:39.070 --> 00:28:41.200
I'm able to then
filter this down

00:28:41.200 --> 00:28:43.960
by different tables
that are being accessed.

00:28:43.960 --> 00:28:47.140
I can even filter this
down by hour of day.

00:28:47.140 --> 00:28:52.120
So I guess I'm active
24/7, sometimes.

00:28:52.120 --> 00:28:55.600
And you're able to also
filter this down by user ID.

00:28:55.600 --> 00:28:58.630
So if I wanted to filter
this down and just see

00:28:58.630 --> 00:29:02.770
the logs that are the
queries that I'm running,

00:29:02.770 --> 00:29:04.120
I can filter that down.

00:29:04.120 --> 00:29:07.540
I could see the estimated
cost of all the queries

00:29:07.540 --> 00:29:08.507
that I've executed.

00:29:08.507 --> 00:29:10.090
And then I can go
in here, and I could

00:29:10.090 --> 00:29:12.520
see these are all the
queries that have executed.

00:29:12.520 --> 00:29:14.650
Maybe potentially,
there's a query in there

00:29:14.650 --> 00:29:17.950
that is substantially more
expensive than the others.

00:29:17.950 --> 00:29:21.370
I can then take a look
at that, and analyze it,

00:29:21.370 --> 00:29:23.460
and see if there's a
way to optimize it.

00:29:23.460 --> 00:29:26.720
And when you have an
organization of end users,

00:29:26.720 --> 00:29:29.050
they may not always write
the most efficient queries.

00:29:29.050 --> 00:29:31.000
So visualizing your
audit logs in this way

00:29:31.000 --> 00:29:34.720
could enable you to be
proactive about tracking down

00:29:34.720 --> 00:29:38.320
those expensive queries and then
informing those users of better

00:29:38.320 --> 00:29:42.670
ways to run them and also
track those costs per user

00:29:42.670 --> 00:29:44.288
per project.

00:29:44.288 --> 00:29:46.264
Back to the slides.

00:29:52.390 --> 00:29:52.890
All right.

00:29:52.890 --> 00:29:55.380
So that's a little bit about
BigQuery best practices.

00:29:55.380 --> 00:29:58.080
But now, Alban from Teads
is going to come up on stage

00:29:58.080 --> 00:30:01.380
and talk a little bit about
how Teads utilized BigQuery

00:30:01.380 --> 00:30:04.100
within their organization.

00:30:04.100 --> 00:30:06.440
ALBAN PERILLAT-MERCEROZ:
Thank you, Ryan.

00:30:06.440 --> 00:30:09.770
Three years ago, I
moved from San Francisco

00:30:09.770 --> 00:30:12.290
to Montpellier South of France.

00:30:12.290 --> 00:30:15.680
I joined Teads in Montpellier
and had the privilege

00:30:15.680 --> 00:30:20.780
to work on a project called
BigQuery POC at the time.

00:30:20.780 --> 00:30:25.790
Today, I am thrilled to be back
in San Francisco to showcase

00:30:25.790 --> 00:30:30.210
how BigQuery has grown
from a proof of concept

00:30:30.210 --> 00:30:33.600
to our main data
warehousing system.

00:30:33.600 --> 00:30:36.930
I'll start with an overview
of how we use BigQuery.

00:30:36.930 --> 00:30:40.440
I'll explain why
we chose flat rate

00:30:40.440 --> 00:30:44.340
and how we manage our workloads,
and will finish by the impact

00:30:44.340 --> 00:30:46.110
that BigQuery has had.

00:30:46.110 --> 00:30:49.380
But first, a few
words about Teads.

00:30:49.380 --> 00:30:51.900
Anyone heard about Teads?

00:30:51.900 --> 00:30:55.110
Not a lot, although
I'm pretty sure

00:30:55.110 --> 00:31:00.370
you regularly see ads served by
Teads on these news publishers.

00:31:00.370 --> 00:31:02.550
So we are an ad tech company.

00:31:02.550 --> 00:31:06.240
We have united the best
publishers in the world

00:31:06.240 --> 00:31:09.540
into this global media platform.

00:31:09.540 --> 00:31:14.040
With Teads, advertisers can
reach 1.4 billion people

00:31:14.040 --> 00:31:15.360
monthly.

00:31:15.360 --> 00:31:21.060
We serve ads at scale
within a premium context.

00:31:21.060 --> 00:31:22.920
Now, why BigQuery?

00:31:22.920 --> 00:31:24.930
This is the kind
of user interface

00:31:24.930 --> 00:31:28.290
that we build for our
clients, for publishers

00:31:28.290 --> 00:31:31.300
to monitor their inventory
and monetization,

00:31:31.300 --> 00:31:34.800
and for advertisers to
manage their campaigns.

00:31:34.800 --> 00:31:38.580
It's full of dashboards where
you can see plenty of metrics

00:31:38.580 --> 00:31:42.280
and in which you can
explore multiple dimensions.

00:31:42.280 --> 00:31:45.760
We want a fast and
interactive experience.

00:31:45.760 --> 00:31:49.510
And to do that, we need to
collect, store, and process

00:31:49.510 --> 00:31:52.360
billions of events.

00:31:52.360 --> 00:31:54.850
Let's take a look at the
sources of these events.

00:31:54.850 --> 00:31:56.680
To do that, we need an ad break.

00:32:07.250 --> 00:32:09.340
So what happened here?

00:32:09.340 --> 00:32:12.410
This ad experience
emitted five events

00:32:12.410 --> 00:32:14.480
so we can know what's going on.

00:32:14.480 --> 00:32:17.230
The first one is
Ad Slot Available.

00:32:17.230 --> 00:32:21.730
That's when there's actually
room for an ad on the web page.

00:32:21.730 --> 00:32:24.170
Then we have Ad Available.

00:32:24.170 --> 00:32:27.070
This is the result of a
complex auction system that

00:32:27.070 --> 00:32:29.260
is running behind
the scenes to select

00:32:29.260 --> 00:32:33.820
the right ad for the right
person at the right price.

00:32:33.820 --> 00:32:37.360
Impression is when the
ad is actually displayed.

00:32:37.360 --> 00:32:39.730
Then User Click
and Video Complete

00:32:39.730 --> 00:32:44.610
are useful metrics
for advertisers.

00:32:44.610 --> 00:32:46.860
10 billion events a day--

00:32:46.860 --> 00:32:49.560
that's more than 100,000
events per second

00:32:49.560 --> 00:33:01.140
that we have to collect, store,
and process within our systems.

00:33:01.140 --> 00:33:04.200
Another important metric is 10.

00:33:04.200 --> 00:33:08.800
We are only a team of 10 to give
meanings to all these events.

00:33:08.800 --> 00:33:11.160
That's why we are more
than happy to delegate

00:33:11.160 --> 00:33:14.130
to BigQuery the complexity
that comes with that scale.

00:33:18.140 --> 00:33:20.290
Now, let me tell you
where we come from,

00:33:20.290 --> 00:33:22.540
what made us choose
BigQuery at Teads,

00:33:22.540 --> 00:33:26.650
and how it slowly became our
main data warehousing system.

00:33:26.650 --> 00:33:30.430
So all these events are
first stored into Kafka.

00:33:30.430 --> 00:33:32.740
Before BigQuery,
our data pipeline

00:33:32.740 --> 00:33:36.430
was following the principles
of the lambda architecture.

00:33:36.430 --> 00:33:40.030
On the batch layer, we
were storing the events S3.

00:33:40.030 --> 00:33:43.780
And then Spark's drives were
aggregating these events

00:33:43.780 --> 00:33:45.970
into Cassandra tables.

00:33:45.970 --> 00:33:50.500
The S3 layer was doing some of
these aggregations in real time

00:33:50.500 --> 00:33:53.020
using Storm.

00:33:53.020 --> 00:33:54.850
This model was complex.

00:33:54.850 --> 00:33:57.250
We had to maintain
two code bases.

00:33:57.250 --> 00:33:58.990
And it did not scale well.

00:33:58.990 --> 00:34:02.380
The cost of Cassandra was
getting out of control,

00:34:02.380 --> 00:34:05.170
and we were limited
in the features

00:34:05.170 --> 00:34:08.489
that we could build upon it.

00:34:08.489 --> 00:34:12.780
In 2016, we chose BigQuery to
overcome these limitations.

00:34:12.780 --> 00:34:17.460
We also chose to simplify
the data pipeline

00:34:17.460 --> 00:34:20.850
with a single data
flow process to load

00:34:20.850 --> 00:34:23.380
all the events into BigQuery.

00:34:23.380 --> 00:34:27.889
We also chose an ELT approach,
as opposed to an ETL approach,

00:34:27.889 --> 00:34:31.989
meaning we first store all
the events into BigQuery,

00:34:31.989 --> 00:34:36.980
and then use BigQuery only
to process these events.

00:34:36.980 --> 00:34:40.429
Let's have a look at
this ELT. So Dataflow

00:34:40.429 --> 00:34:45.409
is loading all these events
into BigQuery using batch mode,

00:34:45.409 --> 00:34:51.170
and writes one table per
batch every about 30 minutes.

00:34:51.170 --> 00:34:55.760
The events you see in
these tables are unordered.

00:34:55.760 --> 00:35:01.160
Then the ELT BigQuery
takes over here.

00:35:01.160 --> 00:35:07.580
And we do several jobs inside
BigQuery from these tables.

00:35:07.580 --> 00:35:09.570
I'll give you two examples.

00:35:09.570 --> 00:35:13.280
The first one is a simple job
that takes all these events

00:35:13.280 --> 00:35:17.330
and appends them into a
single federated table.

00:35:17.330 --> 00:35:20.840
And this table is very
important for our data analysts

00:35:20.840 --> 00:35:27.470
to run exploration queries
or for debugging purposes.

00:35:27.470 --> 00:35:31.010
The second job I will show
here is the rollup job.

00:35:31.010 --> 00:35:34.020
This job has two purposes.

00:35:34.020 --> 00:35:38.180
First, it groups events
by one table per hour,

00:35:38.180 --> 00:35:41.190
so we can know where
to look at the events.

00:35:41.190 --> 00:35:43.850
And the second
role of this job is

00:35:43.850 --> 00:35:48.970
to aggregate these events so
we can query them much faster.

00:35:48.970 --> 00:35:53.680
Let's take an example with
five events that we have.

00:35:53.680 --> 00:35:56.880
So each of these events
have a timestamp, and ad ID,

00:35:56.880 --> 00:36:01.160
and a website ID, and
the name of the event.

00:36:01.160 --> 00:36:04.540
In this example,
we want to group

00:36:04.540 --> 00:36:08.480
into three dimensions-- the hour
from the timestamp, the ad ID,

00:36:08.480 --> 00:36:09.600
and the website ID.

00:36:09.600 --> 00:36:11.950
And we want to compute
three metrics, which

00:36:11.950 --> 00:36:16.600
are the number of impressions,
of starts, and clicks.

00:36:16.600 --> 00:36:19.600
In this example, we've
grouped these five events

00:36:19.600 --> 00:36:21.190
into two lines.

00:36:21.190 --> 00:36:25.000
So that's a 2.5
compression ratio.

00:36:25.000 --> 00:36:28.030
Whereas, in real life,
our prediction job

00:36:28.030 --> 00:36:32.300
can perform a 70x
compression ratio.

00:36:32.300 --> 00:36:36.920
Now before BigQuery, to do that
at scale, we had to run complex

00:36:36.920 --> 00:36:39.410
Sparks jobs written in Scala.

00:36:39.410 --> 00:36:44.420
With BigQuery, it's been
simplified into this simple SAL

00:36:44.420 --> 00:36:46.120
query.

00:36:46.120 --> 00:36:49.690
As you can see, we are
grouping by our dimensions

00:36:49.690 --> 00:36:55.950
and computing the metrics
in the SELECT statement.

00:36:55.950 --> 00:37:01.500
In production, we have 60
dimensions and 150 metrics.

00:37:01.500 --> 00:37:03.660
I can tell you the
query is much longer,

00:37:03.660 --> 00:37:05.700
but it's not more complex.

00:37:05.700 --> 00:37:09.360
It's just as simple as that.

00:37:09.360 --> 00:37:13.140
In production, this query runs
in 1 minute and 40 seconds,

00:37:13.140 --> 00:37:17.130
despite having to
group by 60 dimensions

00:37:17.130 --> 00:37:20.700
and process 100
million rows per batch.

00:37:20.700 --> 00:37:22.560
And the good thing
is it saturated

00:37:22.560 --> 00:37:26.870
the equivalent of our 3,000
slots for only eight seconds.

00:37:26.870 --> 00:37:30.750
It means we have plenty of
room to run multiple queries

00:37:30.750 --> 00:37:32.220
like this at the same time.

00:37:35.170 --> 00:37:38.950
Speaking of slots, I'll now
explain why we chose flat rate

00:37:38.950 --> 00:37:42.430
and how we manage it.

00:37:42.430 --> 00:37:44.650
The most common pricing
model for BigQuery

00:37:44.650 --> 00:37:46.840
is the on-demand
pricing where you

00:37:46.840 --> 00:37:51.370
pay $5 per terabyte
of data you scan.

00:37:51.370 --> 00:37:54.730
Flat rate is an
alternative pricing model.

00:37:54.730 --> 00:37:58.570
For $40,000 a month, you
get 2,000 dedicated slots,

00:37:58.570 --> 00:38:02.460
regardless of the
amount of data you scan.

00:38:02.460 --> 00:38:04.830
If you're scanning few
enough bytes per month,

00:38:04.830 --> 00:38:06.980
it makes sense to use
the on-demand pricing.

00:38:06.980 --> 00:38:08.100
No capacity planning.

00:38:08.100 --> 00:38:09.120
No commitment.

00:38:09.120 --> 00:38:12.980
You only pay for what you use.

00:38:12.980 --> 00:38:17.370
If your bit reaches $40,000 a
month, you have two options.

00:38:17.370 --> 00:38:20.380
You can either reduce your
usage or optimize your queries

00:38:20.380 --> 00:38:23.990
to reduce your bill,
or you can go flat rate

00:38:23.990 --> 00:38:26.890
and try to make
the most out of it.

00:38:26.890 --> 00:38:29.540
At Teads, we chose
the second option.

00:38:29.540 --> 00:38:32.630
It made us focus
on building value

00:38:32.630 --> 00:38:36.410
from BigQuery's unlimited
power, rather than optimizing

00:38:36.410 --> 00:38:38.240
and cost control.

00:38:38.240 --> 00:38:42.620
It encouraged also other
teams to use BigQuery too.

00:38:42.620 --> 00:38:47.510
In three years, Tead's
costs kept stable,

00:38:47.510 --> 00:38:51.230
and we only had to increase
our slot capacity once

00:38:51.230 --> 00:38:53.750
from 2,000 to 3,000.

00:38:53.750 --> 00:38:56.330
And by the way, a
few days ago Google

00:38:56.330 --> 00:39:01.220
announced that flat rate is
available from 500 slots.

00:39:01.220 --> 00:39:09.010
So the entry price is now
about $10,000 a month.

00:39:09.010 --> 00:39:13.990
One of the challenges of flat
rate is workload management.

00:39:13.990 --> 00:39:16.360
With flat rate
pricing, you're not

00:39:16.360 --> 00:39:18.880
counting the bytes you're
scanned by your query.

00:39:18.880 --> 00:39:21.850
Your monitoring your slot usage.

00:39:21.850 --> 00:39:23.890
At some point, you'll
reach saturation

00:39:23.890 --> 00:39:25.550
of your compute capacity.

00:39:25.550 --> 00:39:26.680
That's OK.

00:39:26.680 --> 00:39:29.300
That means you're getting
the most out of BigQuery.

00:39:29.300 --> 00:39:32.170
But you have to make sure
some of your workloads

00:39:32.170 --> 00:39:35.380
are not impacted to
match the others.

00:39:35.380 --> 00:39:39.660
You have to make sure they
are not starving the others.

00:39:39.660 --> 00:39:44.430
Throwing money at the problem
is always the easiest solution.

00:39:44.430 --> 00:39:47.760
So it helps that it's not
a silver bullet, as you

00:39:47.760 --> 00:39:52.550
may end up starving all--

00:39:55.280 --> 00:39:57.770
you may end up saturating
all your slots.

00:39:57.770 --> 00:40:00.470
And yes, you still
have no guarantee

00:40:00.470 --> 00:40:04.250
that some of the workloads
are not starving the others.

00:40:07.530 --> 00:40:10.420
Splitting different workloads
into different projects

00:40:10.420 --> 00:40:13.240
helps the BigQuery
scheduler give a fair share

00:40:13.240 --> 00:40:16.030
of slots for each project.

00:40:16.030 --> 00:40:21.400
This way, a given project cannot
impact too much the others.

00:40:21.400 --> 00:40:23.920
In this example,
the orange project

00:40:23.920 --> 00:40:25.690
is using most of the slots.

00:40:25.690 --> 00:40:28.570
But when the green and
blue project needs some,

00:40:28.570 --> 00:40:32.220
they can access a
fair share of it.

00:40:32.220 --> 00:40:35.070
Another way to
isolate workloads is

00:40:35.070 --> 00:40:39.000
to implement
heirarchical reservation.

00:40:39.000 --> 00:40:41.910
With reservation system,
you can assign slots

00:40:41.910 --> 00:40:45.240
to nodes that are
groups of projects.

00:40:45.240 --> 00:40:49.800
And you can make the slot
sharing uni-directional.

00:40:49.800 --> 00:40:53.700
In this example, we've isolated
the most critical projects

00:40:53.700 --> 00:40:56.250
into the critical node.

00:40:56.250 --> 00:40:59.850
And they get 1,000
dedicated slots.

00:40:59.850 --> 00:41:03.300
They can also access the 2,000
slots of the other project.

00:41:03.300 --> 00:41:06.390
Whereas, the other
projects cannot have access

00:41:06.390 --> 00:41:07.590
to the critical one.

00:41:07.590 --> 00:41:10.890
This makes sure that
critical projects always

00:41:10.890 --> 00:41:14.451
have a minimum of
resource available.

00:41:17.280 --> 00:41:20.320
Now that I've showcased
how we use BigQuery,

00:41:20.320 --> 00:41:26.915
I'll finish by the three major
impacts that it had at Teads.

00:41:26.915 --> 00:41:30.570
It made us build projects
and features that we were not

00:41:30.570 --> 00:41:33.790
able to do before.

00:41:33.790 --> 00:41:38.380
It made data accessible
by everyone easily.

00:41:38.380 --> 00:41:41.380
Thanks to SQL, which is a
game changer when you compare

00:41:41.380 --> 00:41:44.440
it to Jupyter notebooks,
and because thanks

00:41:44.440 --> 00:41:49.060
to a UI with single sign
on and built-in security.

00:41:49.060 --> 00:41:51.340
And last but not
least, it made us

00:41:51.340 --> 00:41:56.200
focus on building projects and
not maintaining infrastructure.

00:41:56.200 --> 00:41:58.390
We use AWS Redshift 2.

00:41:58.390 --> 00:42:01.660
And to give you an idea, we
spend more time operating

00:42:01.660 --> 00:42:03.790
clusters of hundreds
of gigabytes on it

00:42:03.790 --> 00:42:06.250
than we spend time
operating BigQuery

00:42:06.250 --> 00:42:07.840
with hundreds of terabytes.

00:42:10.780 --> 00:42:15.550
Key takeaways-- BigQuery gave
us performance and simplicity.

00:42:15.550 --> 00:42:18.810
It made our data accessible.

00:42:18.810 --> 00:42:24.370
Flat rate drives usage
with predictable costs that

00:42:24.370 --> 00:42:26.371
requires workload management.

00:42:29.020 --> 00:42:31.750
If you want to know more
about our data pipeline,

00:42:31.750 --> 00:42:35.920
our engineering blog has
phenomenal articles about it.

00:42:35.920 --> 00:42:37.270
Honestly, they are great.

00:42:37.270 --> 00:42:41.390
And I'm not only saying
that because I wrote them.

00:42:41.390 --> 00:42:43.850
Finally, if like me
you prefer sunny south

00:42:43.850 --> 00:42:47.210
of France over the 101
commutes, we are hiring.

00:42:47.210 --> 00:42:47.810
[APPLAUSE]

00:42:47.810 --> 00:42:50.560
